Koda, an AI coding agent for your terminal.

Run `koda` to start an interactive session, or `koda exec` to run one prompt and exit.

Usage: koda [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]
       koda [OPTIONS] <COMMAND> [ARGS]

Commands:
  exec       Run one prompt without an interactive session, then exit
  resume     Resume a saved session (pick from a list, or --last for the most recent)
  fork       Copy a saved session and continue in the copy (pick from a list, or --last)
  archive    Archive a saved session by id
  unarchive  Unarchive a saved session by id
  mcp        Manage the MCP servers every session starts
  serve      Run the background server that interactive sessions use
  app        Open Koda's desktop app (macOS)
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PROMPT]
          Prompt to start the interactive session with

Options:
  -m, --model <MODEL>
          Model to use: an id, or an alias such as `claude-opus`

      --provider <PROVIDER>
          Provider: anthropic, openai, gemini, groq, grok, ollama, lm-studio or llama-cpp

      --base-url <URL>
          Provider endpoint, for a local server or a gateway

      --oss
          Run on a local model that Koda starts with llama.cpp.
          
          The model is Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on a Mac with 128 GB of memory, and LFM3-1.7B (a 1 GB
          download, for a quick try) elsewhere; set `oss_model` in config.toml to choose another.
          Koda stops the model server when it exits.

      --local-provider <PROVIDER>
          Local provider for --oss: llama-cpp (the default; Koda starts it), lm-studio or ollama.
          
          lm-studio and ollama must already be running.

      --reasoning-effort <LEVEL>
          How much the model reasons: low, medium, high, xhigh or max.
          
          Claude models take these levels, and each has its own default. For an OpenAI-compatible
          server the value is sent as `reasoning_effort`.
          
          [alias: --effort]

      --mode <MODE>
          What Koda may do without asking: auto (the default) or plan.
          
          auto: commands run without asking, inside the sandbox. A command that needs to leave the
          sandbox, a dangerous one (such as rm -rf or git push --force) and a file outside the
          project ask first. With no sandbox on this machine, every command asks.
          
          plan: read only. Commands run in a read-only sandbox; Koda looks around, proposes a plan,
          and asks before carrying it out.
          
          config.toml's `mode` sets the default.
          
          [possible values: auto, plan]

  -i, --image <FILE>
          Attach images to the first prompt (repeatable, or comma-separated)

  -C, --cd <DIR>
          Directory to work in (default: the current directory)
          
          [alias: --project-root]

      --worktree
          Work in a new Git worktree of the project.
          
          The worktree is a detached checkout of HEAD, outside the repository, under
          ~/.config/koda/worktrees/. It stays after Koda exits; remove it with `git worktree remove
          <path>`. With `koda fork <id>`, the copy opens in a new worktree of the repository that
          session worked in.

  -c, --config <KEY=VALUE>
          Set a config.toml value for this run.
          
          Use a dotted key for a nested value, for example `-c
          model_providers.lm-studio.base_url=http://localhost:1234/v1`. The value is read as TOML,
          or as plain text if it isn't valid TOML. Repeatable; nothing is saved. A flag that sets
          the same value, such as --model, wins.

      --mcp-config <FILE|JSON>
          Add MCP servers for this run only, from a JSON file or inline JSON.
          
          Use the `{"mcpServers": {...}}` format of `.mcp.json` files. Repeatable; a later server
          wins a name clash, and these win over servers added with `koda mcp add`. Nothing is saved.

  -a, --agent <AGENT>
          Agent to run: a built-in one, or a JSON file in .koda/agents/ or ~/.config/koda/agents/.
          
          The default is config.toml's `agent`, else `default`.

      --max-context-tokens <TOKENS>
          Context window size in tokens, for a server that doesn't report one.
          
          Overrides the agent file, the server's report and Koda's model table.

  -s, --resume <SESSION>
          Resume a saved session by id (as `koda resume <id>` does)

      --no-daemon
          Run in this process instead of on the background server.
          
          By default an interactive session runs on `koda serve`, which Koda starts when needed, so
          a session goes on after its window closes.

      --remote <ADDR>
          Connect to a background server at this address (unix://PATH).
          
          Koda doesn't start or replace a server given this way.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  koda                               Start an interactive session
  koda "fix the failing test"        Start one with a first prompt
  koda resume --last                 Continue the most recent session
  koda exec "explain this codebase"  Run one prompt and print the reply
  git diff | koda exec               Read the prompt from stdin

Documentation: https://lijunzh.github.io/koda/
